WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.630
- All right, I'm gonna
go ahead and get started

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.270
as more people filter in.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.157
Thank you so much for joining us for the

00:00:17.157 --> 00:00:19.560
"Urgency for Action at COP26:

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.680
What Will a Fair Deal Look Like?

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.570
This is the ninth in the series,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.970
The Yale Development Dialogues,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.940
which is a series of
virtual panel discussions

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.050
hosted by Yale's Economic Growth Center,

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.040
The Jackson Institute for Global Affairs

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.030
and also Yale's History Department.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.010
I'm Catherine Cheney.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.750
I'm a journalist focused on development,

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.780
and I'm thrilled to moderate
these conversations,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.500
looking to lessons from
history and economics,

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.480
as well as policy and
practice to address today's

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.130
policy challenges in low
and middle income countries.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.320
Today, I'm joined by several panelists.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.510
I'll quickly introduce them,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.810
and then I'll let them go
into more depth about their

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.350
connection with this topic.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.230
John Haldon is a professor
at Princeton University who

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.330
directs the Climate Change and
History Research Initiative,

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.750
which is an interdisciplinary
project that investigates

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.320
the societal impact of climatic
changes across the last two

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.640
millennia.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.000
Sunita Narain is a writer
and environmentalist

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.910
who is Director General of the India based

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:16.830
Research Institute for
Science and Environment,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.180
as well as editor of Down to Earth.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.020
Nicholas Ryan is an Assistant
Professor of Economics at Yale

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.960
whose work examines
energy policy in India.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.350
and Rohini Pande is Henry J
Heinz, The Second Professor of

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.140
Economics and Director of
the Economic Growth Center

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.300
at Yale.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.860
So before I hand it over to our panelists,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.800
I just wanna provide a
little bit of context

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.160
and I'll ask Rohini to
expand on this in a moment,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.880
as she's really helped to
spearhead this session.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.720
But according to a report
from Oxfam, released earlier

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.070
this year, the richest 1%
of the global population

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.920
have used two times as much
carbon as the poorest 50%

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.900
over the last 25 years.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.090
Now, climate is the top
threat to public health

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.910
and its impacts on humanity from droughts

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.570
to rising sea levels to
worsening air pollution,

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.800
will hit the poorest the hardest.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.550
Some wealthy nations are calling
for lower income countries

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.550
to pursue renewable energy
rather than fossil fuels,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.860
even as they themselves
continue to rely on those

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.270
sources of energy.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.050
That leads some to call
for a just transition.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.090
And that's what we'll be discussing today.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.000
I was tuning into some
of the sessions at the

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.400
United Nations General Assembly recently,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.470
and was struck by what the
Prime Minister of Barbados

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.990
had to say and really
thought it related to this

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:28.823
conversation.

00:02:28.823 --> 00:02:30.460
So here's just a quote from her.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.010
She said, "How much more
must sea levels climb in

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.060
small island developing states
before those who profited

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.360
from the stockpiling of greenhouse
gases, contribute to the

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.660
loss and damage they have
occasioned, rather than asking us

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.290
to crowd out the fiscal space
that we have for development

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.140
to cure the damage caused
by the greed of others?"

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.310
So that's just one example of
where there's a real source of

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.060
tension when it comes to
who is being asked to make

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.800
what commitments as we
look ahead into COP26?

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.470
So today we'll focus on the commitments of

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.410
high and low income countries
to address climate change

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:07.690
in the context of the
upcoming United Nations

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.680
Climate Change Conference
of the Parties or COP26,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.580
taking place in Glasgow from
October 31st to November 12th.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.960
And our panelists will
discuss climate policy from

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.110
a lower income country perspective,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.080
and also consider who is
responsible for bearing the costs

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.980
of reducing emissions.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.580
For those of you joining on
Zoom, we really wanna make this

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.413
a conversation.

00:03:27.413 --> 00:03:28.770
We welcome your questions.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.060
So please use that Q&amp;A function in Zoom,

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.010
keep those questions coming
and I'll get to as many

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:33.843
as I can.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:36.880
But without further ado, I
wanna bring our panelists

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.300
into this, and we'll start with Rohini.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.560
So before we get to COP26 specifically,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.800
I just wanna ask each of
our panelists to expand

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.440
on how their work
informs their perspective

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.310
on this intersection of climate
change and social justice.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.540
So I briefly introduced
you all, but Rohini,

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.613
can you expand on where
you come at this question?

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.430
- So let me start by noting
the fact that the Nobel Prize

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.960
in physics today went for
work that will seem to be

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.070
important for climate change.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.430
And I think a lot of initial
work and thinking about issues

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:13.360
of climate change has come from
science or from engineering

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.090
and thinking about issues
to do with technology.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:19.790
What I've been interested in
is asking what perspective can

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.870
economist, especially
those who think about

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.970
implementation issues and the
political economy bring to it.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.760
And I think repeatedly I've
seen in much of my work,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.760
some of it with Nick, is that
we feel when we don't think of

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:38.520
designing institutions and
policies that take into account

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.430
both what citizens want, but importantly,

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.730
what it is feasible given
the institutional structure

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:44.950
in that place.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:47.050
And so looking ahead to COP26,

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.090
I know there'll be many points
where lawyers are having

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.300
discussions and then many points
where leaders of countries

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.860
will have discussions.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.720
And what I would love to
see is some more discussion,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.720
of, how will you translate
any policies you come up with

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.860
or any targets you come
up with on the ground,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:10.170
given the constraints that
exist and what does that mean

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:11.330
for what would happen?

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.010
So that's really the
perspective I'd love to bring

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:14.380
Catherine.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.350
- Thank you Rohini.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.800
And I agree, we'll make sure
that we talk about policies

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.240
and their impacts on the ground today.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.860
I wanna bring John into this
and I'll note that when John

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.400
and I were first interacting
about the possibility of him

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.700
joining us as a panelist,
he talked about how his lens

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:34.380
on history goes way back,
which he can tell us a bit more

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.440
about in a moment.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.030
And he wasn't sure, was
that the right addition

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:38.863
to this conversation?

00:05:38.863 --> 00:05:41.090
Or do we want someone focused
on more recent history?

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.940
I just wanna say I'm thrilled
to have your perspective

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:44.773
looking way back.

00:05:44.773 --> 00:05:47.500
And I know some of the
points that you're gonna make

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.250
from a long time ago are
gonna be hugely relevant to

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.010
discussions on what's coming up at COP26.

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.450
So John, tell us a little
bit more about your work

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.620
and how that informed
your perspective on this

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.073
climate change, social
justice intersection.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:01.750
- Thank you, Catherine.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.730
And thank you to the organizers
for inviting me to be part

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.430
of this conversation.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:10.340
I think the big question is,
can history tell us anything

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:13.300
about how to address
contemporary problems?

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:16.490
And the short answer is, yes, it can,

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:19.430
but probably not in the
way that one expects it to

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.970
be able to do.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:25.080
So, the first thing is that
when one looks at the past,

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.570
whether it's the deep past,
let's take neolithic societies

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:31.960
for example, about which we
can say, quite a lot through

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:36.400
the archeological record
and paleo climatic records,

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:39.430
stretching right up to
recent pre-modern times,

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.460
when one looks at these
different societies,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:46.110
one thing stands out and
that is that resilience

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.840
and sustainability has been
a hallmark of human societies

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.980
from the very earliest times.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.480
And indeed, most societies
have shown a remarkable degree

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.493
of resilience against even the
harshest sorts of challenges.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.700
What tends to make a crucial
difference is a societal

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.740
structure and internal
social relationships,

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.810
but crucially belief systems
and cultural attitudes,

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.090
these are really, really important.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:19.800
And I think they're one of
the things that when one looks

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:24.750
for example, the UN list of
SDG, sustainable development

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:27.950
goals, one of the things
that doesn't really have much

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.050
presence there is attitudes,
attitudes to change,

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:36.140
attitudes to challenges,
attitudes to social and economic

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:38.080
relationships and so forth.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.880
They're there, they're implicit,
but they're not addressed

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.230
explicitly and they need to be.

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:46.590
And the historical record
and the archeological record

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.513
can actually tell us
quite a lot about that.

00:07:49.513 --> 00:07:52.710
And that seems to me to be a
very significant contribution

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.093
that looking at the past can make.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:01.060
- I love that, and your
point on attitudes, implicit,

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.450
but maybe not addressed in
the way they should be in the

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:05.283
SDGs.

00:08:05.283 --> 00:08:07.520
I think that's well taken and
the same could be said for

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.150
climate policy attitudes and
the importance of attitudes.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:14.932
I'd love to go to Sunita
next, if that's all right.

00:08:14.932 --> 00:08:19.090
Sunita I know you're working
day in, day out on these issues

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:21.230
and that it's late your time.

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.720
So we really appreciate you joining us,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.490
but can you talk a little
bit more about your work,

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:30.270
which I only briefly previewed
and basically what is your

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.540
key message or the things
you wanna get into today

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:36.193
when it comes to what a fair
deal looks like at COP26?

00:08:37.535 --> 00:08:41.270
- Yeah, Catherine, I mean,
John, gave us an amazing

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:42.103
beginning.

00:08:42.103 --> 00:08:45.940
And for me, history,
doesn't go back that long,

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:48.300
but a little bit of recent history.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.840
I was in Rio when the
Framework Convention on Climate

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.350
Change was signed.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:58.350
And I was there because we had
my colleague, he passed away,

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.860
he and I actually worked
on a publication called

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:07.030
Global Warming in an Unequal
World: A Case of Environmental

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.270
Colonialism.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.670
And this was 1991.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:14.680
And the report essentially was
a report, which argued that,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:19.210
if you did not have a fair
and equitable agreement,

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:23.570
you could never have any
effective and ambitious agreement.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:28.570
And unfortunately, Catherine,
over the last 30 odd years,

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:33.960
we have seen the pushback has
always been to try and erase

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.750
the concept of equity.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:40.690
And to a large extent,
we did succeed in Paris.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:45.690
But the fact of the matter is
it is real, you can't really

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:48.210
wipe out something that is real.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:53.210
And it's real because even if
the rich polluted in the past,

00:09:55.562 --> 00:09:57.810
the poor will pollute in the future.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.770
And so unless you have a
collaborative agreement

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:05.990
and one which everybody thinks
is fair, believes is fair,

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:09.560
you will not have an
agreement which is effective.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.560
And that actually came, and
just to add the reason we did

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:20.170
this report and actually
that work was because we were

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.010
working in India, and the
work in India, and I've always

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.640
said this and I've written
it, that our work on

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.260
climate change was very
influenced by our work on forestry

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:35.260
in India, where we understood
that the goats which people

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:40.300
kept in villages would eat up
any forest unless the people

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:44.610
who had the goats knew that
they would get benefits from

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.060
the common forests that existed.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.370
And subsequently all the
work that we have done,

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:53.250
and I continue to do, whether
it's on air pollution,

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:58.250
where Nick has done a lot of
work, or on excreter and water,

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:02.800
because our work as much
more, my work is in India.

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:07.800
We have found the abiding
principle has been, that if it is

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.310
not affordable growth, if
it is not inclusive growth,

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.730
it cannot be sustainable growth.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:17.650
In India, our biggest issue
with air pollution is because

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:20.160
we do not have mobility for all.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.710
And unless we have mobility
for all, we will never have

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:24.543
clean air.

00:11:24.543 --> 00:11:28.120
Unless we have clean energy
for all, we will never have

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:28.953
clean air.

00:11:28.953 --> 00:11:31.110
So the air shared is
one, and that's something

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.830
we have learned because of
the horrendous pollution

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.330
that we have in our cities.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:39.890
But it is very much the same
story on climate change.

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:44.890
So my look has been influenced
by both what I know in India,

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:48.800
at the global level and
also the frustration at the

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.580
global level has only
made us believe that,

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:57.030
understand that we need to do
much more to make the right

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.840
policies in our own countries.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:00.673
- Thank you, Sunita.

00:12:00.673 --> 00:12:02.470
And we'll get into what that looks like.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:03.550
Just a couple reactions.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.890
One thing I hope to get
to in our conversation,

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:09.850
when you talked about trying
to erase the concept of equity,

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:14.530
is how COVID-19 has really,
I think reintroduced the

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:17.200
concept of equity to
our global conversation

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:18.630
and the conversations on climate change.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.620
So that's something I wanna
hear from our panelists on,

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:21.870
in a little bit.

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:24.270
But first I do wanna bring Nick
into this, and you mentioned

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.740
Nick's work in India and on air pollution.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.590
And I do think I'm thrilled
to have Nick's perspective

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:33.440
because air pollution is
a very concrete way to

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.010
ground this all in an
example of how climate change

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:39.580
affects people's daily lives
and how policies can benefit

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:44.240
people in their daily lives
versus these conversations at

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.400
COP26, that often can feel
removed from people's day to day.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:50.240
So, Nick, can you talk a
little bit more about your work

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:52.160
in India and how it
informs your perspective

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:53.460
on this question?

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.190
- Sure, so I study energy markets and also

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:57.950
environmental regulation.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:02.450
And I think a goal of my
work is to try to break down

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:06.213
this idea that everything is zero sum.

00:13:06.213 --> 00:13:09.440
A zero sum is the idea
that, if you regulated the

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.690
environment, it's going to be
costly and that will slow down

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:17.690
industrial growth or
that will inhibit social

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:19.390
and political development.

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:23.290
And the way that I sort of
tried to do that is studying

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:26.900
markets where there's some
feature that doesn't work

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.680
very well apart from just
the environmental harm

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:32.510
and see how that could be improved.

00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.660
So for example, one of the
great triumphs in energy access

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.570
have been that in the last 10 years or so,

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:43.200
almost all of rural India
has been electrified.

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.490
And if you look at that from
a climate point of view,

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:49.810
people in developed countries
sometimes have reservations

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:52.180
about that, that very poor
people are getting on the

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.870
electricity grid for the first time.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.910
But I think that that's efficient
and the right investment

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:02.530
to be made and that's happening
in part because of things

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:05.410
like drops in the prices of
renewable energy that make it

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:07.820
more affordable to
supply grid electricity.

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:11.350
So there are cases like this,
not to say that they're the

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:16.050
only or the dominant case, where
you can get win-win kind of

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.410
situation, where there's
investment, that investment

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:22.760
is sponsored in part by imported
solar panels coming from

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:26.070
China or technological developments
that were originally due

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:29.290
to scientists working all over the world,

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:32.990
but it actually brings energy
to poor people rather than

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:33.960
denying it.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:37.360
And I think to me that's
a very important point

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:41.990
and occurs sort of paper
in 1991 was very far

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:45.230
ahead of its time, in terms of,

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.580
there is no progress without equity

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:52.040
and what you've seen now,
the transition from old

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.960
Kyoto system to the now post Paris system

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.130
is a system where countries
put forward their own targets.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:01.680
And so it was exactly an
institutional acknowledgement,

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:06.210
albeit 20 odd years late, 20
odd years behind this paper

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:08.710
that we're not going to
make any progress unless

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.440
countries are making their own
pledges and trying to stick

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:14.440
by those for their own motivations,

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:16.490
which would include economic development.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:18.810
- Thank you, Nick.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:22.740
I wanna stick on the topic of
air pollution for a moment.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:26.790
And speaking of the impacts
of COVID-19 on this space,

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.210
as we all know, lockdowns
led to improve air quality,

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.250
outdoor air quality and kind
of raised awareness of the

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:37.710
kinds of drastic changes that
will be needed for us to see

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.000
an impact in our surroundings.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.670
But the pandemic also brought
renewed attention to the issue

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:44.390
of indoor air pollution.

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:48.390
So I just wonder if any of our
panelists want to expand on

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:52.420
why now might be a unique
window of opportunity to tackle

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:56.540
the issue of air pollution and
how, again that connects to

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:58.063
what a fair deal looks like?

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.230
Maybe Rohini, do you
wanna jump in on this?

00:16:02.340 --> 00:16:04.690
- Sure, I'm not sure
COVID necessarily makes me

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.710
very optimistic about global
action, because in some of what

00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:11.970
we see is a lot of inward
looking nationalism on vaccines

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:13.940
and those issues.

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:16.670
Having said that, of course,
at an individual level,

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:20.970
it's a point in time where
people may find its way to change

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.230
behavior that improves outcomes.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:28.220
But I think one interesting
fact that, a student I know

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:31.840
is working on, is really
this question of how do you

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:36.010
trade off individual activism
with system level change?

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.670
So I think, and I think COP26
is a good point to reflect

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:42.140
on this, that you very
often have people making

00:16:42.140 --> 00:16:44.340
individual pledges like,
"I will travel less,"

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:47.490
or "I will not eat meat."

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:50.390
But perhaps those changes,
sometimes you do that

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:52.070
and then you believe you
don't actually need to engage

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:54.530
in activism or you don't
need to engage in larger

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:57.710
system change because
you've made your trade off.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.260
But we know that that's
not going to be enough.

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:03.500
So I think one thing, the
two things about COVID

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.490
that I would highlight is,
one is I think made countries

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.310
very aware that they have
to look out for their own

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:12.253
interests and I don't know
how that's going to play out.

00:17:12.253 --> 00:17:14.750
And I'd be curious to
hear what boundless thing

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:15.990
that will do.

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:19.290
And then the other I think
is, it's made people aware

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:22.070
of their own choices and
whether that's actually going to

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:24.400
push them to make, even
push for a lot of change

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:27.750
at the system level or feel
like, "I've done my part,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:28.767
I can rest there."

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.170
- Okay, two great points, I'd
love to hear reactions to.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:37.380
And John, I'd also love to hear
how you think history plays

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:38.213
into this.

00:17:38.213 --> 00:17:41.800
But when Rohini outlines,
COVID has made people aware of

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:45.050
their own choices, COVID has
made countries aware they have

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:48.520
to look after their own
interests, actually I mean,

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:50.380
can we go in history a little bit, John?

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:52.290
And I know this is something
you and I discussed in

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:55.420
preparation for this discussion,

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:59.320
what history can teach us
as we're sort of at this

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:00.490
crossroads.

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:03.670
And then we'll kind of get
into the impact of COVID

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:06.580
and bring a Sunita again into this.

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:10.240
- Yeah, I mean, I think the
real issue is motivating people

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.100
to want to make changes.

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:15.490
So as Rohini said, you can
get individuals certainly

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:20.120
at certain levels within a
society to make family based

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:25.120
or individual choice changes,
which impact their own lives

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:28.360
directly and they're willing
to make those changes.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.630
But unless there is a very
substantial cumulative impact,

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:36.070
they at least to begin with
appear to have very little

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:37.440
overall effect.

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:40.390
But it's the motivation aspect
that I think is interesting.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:45.390
Certainly looking at the
past when people are directly

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:49.120
threatened or challenged they'll
act and they often act very

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.830
quickly and very effectively.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:53.900
And that goes right back to
ancient times where you can see

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:58.900
responses to floods,
cataclysmic climate events,

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:01.070
and so forth.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:05.933
When you get rapid visible
impact, people respond to them,

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:11.830
not always effectively, but
often enough to enable survival

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:17.573
and sustainable economic
life after the impact.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.960
The cost of course varies
according to context,

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:24.250
sometimes the cost can be
enormous involving great

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:27.320
loss of life, even though
the society as a whole might

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:28.680
come through it.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:31.340
On other occasions where some degree of

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:35.100
infrastructural planning for,
for example, flooding has

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:38.400
taken place and there are plenty
of cases where that can be

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:43.400
demonstrated very clearly,
the losses can be minimized.

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.680
There's a short-term hit,
but the overall impact

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:47.803
can be minimized.

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:52.810
So motivation, then I suppose
what I'm saying is depends on

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:57.660
how directly it affects the
individuals or the communities

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:58.810
in question.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:02.130
But then there's also the
point at which cultural

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:04.100
and belief systems intervene.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:06.480
Because you can have a very direct impact

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:10.230
and people will continue to
deny the reality of the impact

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.907
when it comes to taking direct action.

00:20:12.907 --> 00:20:16.730
And we can see that today in
communities which are hit,

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:21.690
for example, by extraordinarily
high summer temperatures

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:24.840
causing wildfires and
substantial economic and

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.290
infrastructural damage.

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:28.640
And yet there are still elements
of those communities who

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:32.160
deny that has anything to do
with global climate change

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:34.483
or global climate change impacts.

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:41.410
In the past, what tends to
happen, and it seems to be

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:45.980
a general rule is that the
people who are best able to

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:50.800
weather serious dramatic
challenges are those with the best

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:55.800
access to resources, i.e.
the social and wealth elites.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:58.880
How one defines those
elites varies by social

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:01.500
and economic system of course.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:05.300
They don't have to be narrow
groups at the top of the

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:06.133
pyramid as it were.

00:21:06.133 --> 00:21:09.783
They could be much more
substantial numbers of people.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:15.240
But motivating those elites
to change, tends to be much

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:20.240
harder because their whole
life system, if you like,

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:23.600
the basis for their situation in society,

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:27.210
and more generally depends
upon the status quo,

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:30.540
and asking them to alter the
status quo in a way which will

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:35.540
directly impact their own
opportunities is really very

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:37.233
problematic.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:41.370
So there are two problems
really, the people who have

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:44.680
least invested in a society,
i.e. the people who are most

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:49.680
underprivileged tend to be
those who are also least willing

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:53.340
to make changes because they see no point.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:56.090
And at the other end, you've
got the people who won't make

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:59.670
changes because they see the
potential damage it will do

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.450
to their own situation.

00:22:01.450 --> 00:22:04.970
And as a historian, I really
don't have an answer to how

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:07.980
to deal with either of those issues.

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:11.763
I suspect the people who
are least privileged,

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:17.120
with least access to resources
are going to be easier

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:21.210
to persuade, to help make
changes than those who have

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:24.270
most to lose, which are the very wealthy

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:26.950
and the social elites.

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:29.690
Again, I want to be clear
that we have to define elites

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:30.890
quite carefully.

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:34.410
It's not simply those with
most money or those with

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:35.690
most power.

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:38.320
It has to be sociological
a little bit more refined

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:41.600
and nuanced than that,
but you get the point.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:42.433
- Absolutely.

00:22:42.433 --> 00:22:43.870
I thought you put it well
when you talked about people

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:48.470
who benefit from the status
quo, it's hard to encourage

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:51.010
action when that's the case.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:53.270
I wanna bring Sunita into this.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:56.810
So John has kind of talked about
how these dynamics play out

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:58.946
over the course of history.

00:22:58.946 --> 00:23:02.840
You've talked about more
recent history and in your work

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.760
over decades on this issue.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.750
So when it comes to motivating
change, as Rohini put it

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:13.020
earlier, not just individual activism,

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:16.020
but systems level change,
we need action across

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:16.853
the spectrum.

00:23:16.853 --> 00:23:20.500
And John just outlined why
it's harder to get some people

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:21.430
to act than others.

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:25.040
So Sunita, can you just kind of comment on

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.870
whether you see this as an
opportunity and what the impact

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:30.653
of COVID might be on this space?

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:35.000
- I agree with Rohini.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:39.310
The fact is that COVID is
actually, should really worry us

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:41.520
in terms of global action.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:46.520
I mean, I don't think anyone
of us, at least I never thought

00:23:46.807 --> 00:23:50.070
of, we would see disruption
at the scale that we saw

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:51.540
the last few years.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:53.350
I mean, we were all locked up at home.

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:56.630
Some economies have collapsed,
livelihoods have gone.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:59.500
We're all roaming around with
masks that we would all have

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:02.210
resisted, there are
locked downs of economies.

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:06.750
And yes, in Delhi we saw
blue skies and we celebrated

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:11.560
the sound of birds and we saw
the nature reclaimed itself,

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:15.840
but at the cost, which clearly
is something that people will

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:20.220
today, in my view, on one
hand, we are celebrating

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:23.370
clean air but on the other
hand, people are realizing

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:27.920
that the cost of that was just
so extraordinary that there

00:24:29.021 --> 00:24:30.810
is also a pushed back against it.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:35.810
So and it is a fact that we
have, this is the worst time

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:42.193
in the world, the leadership
in the world literally sucks.

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:47.180
We could not have thought about
a world in which disruption

00:24:47.180 --> 00:24:50.870
of this scale and yet
to have vaccine racism

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:54.400
happening today, yet
to have a divided word.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:56.880
I mean, what does it tell
us about the future of

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:57.720
climate change?

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:01.590
I mean, the good news is
yes, G7 goes and talks about

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:03.360
an interdependent world.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:07.380
They've understood interdependence
that they had never

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:12.380
taught what happened when the
virus moves across nations

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:15.180
and we're all connected.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:17.290
But that's what climate change is about.

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.891
That is exactly what
climate change is about.

00:25:19.891 --> 00:25:23.490
And that is exactly why the
world has to come together

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:25.580
with the leadership that demands.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:27.520
And as John has put it very clearly,

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.880
I mean, it is the poor
who are the victims of

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:32.710
climate change, but they are
the ones who do not have a

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.950
voice in the discussions.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.590
They don't have a voice to
say that they need to do

00:25:37.590 --> 00:25:38.480
something about it.

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:41.240
Let's be clear, they
understand climate change.

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:44.270
I mean, the farmers of
India are not, they don't,

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:47.200
they may not know the fancy
word "climate change,"

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.400
but they understand that
there is something at fault,

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:53.740
that this is serious, they
need to do something about it.

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:58.550
And they make the links with
the way weather is changing,

00:25:58.550 --> 00:26:00.710
that, and if you explain a little more,

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:02.840
they will understand pollution.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:07.160
And yes, I think there
will be a desire to change.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:12.010
But there is a huge, and I
think it's much more to do with

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:17.010
not just the system of the
economy, but it's much more to do

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:21.660
with our inability to
actually do things at scale

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:23.120
which is different.

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:25.930
I mean, that's the opportunity
that I think we have

00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:26.763
in India.

00:26:26.763 --> 00:26:30.080
I mean, when we started
out work on air pollution,

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:34.690
the entire approach was, how
would you fix, and rightly so,

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:37.300
how would you fix the tailpipe of cars?

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:40.520
How would you do better emission control?

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:44.490
But what we realized soon enough is that,

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:49.490
barely 15% of Delhi drives and
there is a common air shared

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:53.170
and that if you have 85% of Delhi driving,

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:56.500
as they do in Washington,
there is no road space,

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:58.100
that is no air shared.

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:01.850
And that is why people in
the US have, their emissions,

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:05.510
their transport related
emissions today, have jeopardized

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:08.390
and brought us on at the
brink of this catastrophe.

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:11.243
So if people in Delhi
have to understand that,

00:27:11.243 --> 00:27:13.890
"Unless we reinvent our mobility system,

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:15.590
we don't get clean air."

00:27:15.590 --> 00:27:19.810
And the transformation
of the mobility system,

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:21.260
has to be managed at scale.

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:25.080
We are still talking about
a few battery vehicles here

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:29.360
and a few buses here and all
that, when CSE worked on its

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:33.410
first generation air pollution
reform, we talked about

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:38.270
moving to compressed natural
gas and a hundred thousand

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:40.890
vehicles moved to gas
in a matter of a year,

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:44.210
that scale of transformation
brought impact.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:47.450
And that's really what we
need in our countries today.

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:48.960
And that's what we need in the world.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:53.400
So, but it's also got to
do with this enormous,

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:57.500
for me, the biggest battle
is get the idea understood

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:00.630
and the possibility of change understood.

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:03.660
Because there is so much
mental apathy to think

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:07.510
of something different and
to think that it is possible.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:11.164
And I think it's a lot to do
with the technology systems,

00:28:11.164 --> 00:28:13.890
the education systems that we have,

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.943
and of course the huge vested interest.

00:28:15.943 --> 00:28:19.610
I mean, moving to gas simple
as that was so difficult

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.930
because of the interest in diesel.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:26.800
Moving out of coal in Delhi
today was possible because

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:29.000
we just pushed it through.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:32.650
But moving out of coal in
our industries is difficult

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:34.190
because one, we don't have reliable power,

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:39.190
and two, it's the cheapest
form of fuel available.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:43.840
So I think it's tough time
in the world Catherine,

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.610
a little longer answer,
but just explain to you,

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:48.263
it's a tough time in the world.

00:28:48.263 --> 00:28:53.263
And COVID has actually pushed
us in my view backwards

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:55.000
rather than forward.

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.590
Because it is going to
be a desperation now.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:00.790
And unfortunately that
desperation with mean,

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:03.560
we would reinvest in the
brown economy rather than

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:06.400
the green, even though
we will talk about green,

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:07.910
but we will go brown.

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:09.970
And that's what we have to fight.

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:14.000
And we have to make sure
that we can call it out.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:18.210
Otherwise we are all in it for,

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:20.450
we'll all be saying the nice sweet words

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:21.560
and nothing would change.

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:23.940
- You're absolutely right.
- I would like...

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:24.773
- Please go ahead, Nick.

00:29:24.773 --> 00:29:27.600
I was just gonna say
quickly that this framing of

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:31.350
investing in the green, not
the brown and the desperation,

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:33.680
could it lead to investment
in the brown not the green,

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:34.730
you have to fight it.

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:37.810
Nick, I think your research
kind of speaks to how.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:40.670
- Well I would just like
to put some numbers.

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:43.520
I think probably two points,

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.660
I very highly share the pessimism
of what COVID has revealed

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.800
about the capability for
international cooperation,

00:29:50.800 --> 00:29:52.450
but to put a number on it.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:57.010
The right order of magnitude
or a number for renewable

00:29:57.010 --> 00:29:59.087
energy investment that's needed
worldwide is something like

00:29:59.087 --> 00:30:00.960
a hundred trillion dollars.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:05.220
So IRENA, for example, says $131 trillion.

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.280
And that number is kind
of inconceivably large.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:10.470
Nobody encounters a
number like that in their

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:11.640
day to day life.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:15.080
But a basis for comparison
would be, how much would it cost

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:18.440
to vaccinate every person
in the world against COVID.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:22.520
And that would be something
like 70 billion, depending on,

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.960
what price you'd negotiate
and the scale, but say there's

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.830
seven billion people and
it's 10 bucks a dose.

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:30.700
EU negotiated $12 a dose,
probably it would be cheaper

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:33.670
than that because, you
could bring it down.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:37.340
So what that means is that the
difference between the scale,

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:39.750
to Sunita's point of
investment that's needed for

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:44.530
climate change versus the scale
that would eliminate COVID,

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:48.460
is about 1,500 times.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:52.710
So that is how much larger the problem is,

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:54.360
just in in money terms.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.900
And that's the kind of reason
to be a little bit pessimistic

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:01.230
if we can't get our act together
and if say the US or China

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.417
unilaterally is not
willing to step up and say,

00:31:03.417 --> 00:31:06.830
"Here's $70 billion to
eliminate COVID effectively

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.613
from the world," then what's the prospect?

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:13.130
Now that's extremely pessimistic.

00:31:13.130 --> 00:31:15.700
I'm not personally quite that pessimistic.

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:18.930
And I think the reason why is
that there have been sources,

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:22.280
there have been movements
in private sector,

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:25.670
especially that bring down
the cost of renewable energy,

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:27.420
bring down the cost of these alternatives.

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:31.140
So even while governments have
been twiddling their thumbs

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:34.190
and sort of not
accomplishing terribly much,

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:37.090
there has been a massive
wave of renewable energy

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:40.290
investment, which is in part
accelerated this sort of

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:42.480
adoption of electricity and other fuels

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:43.410
in developing countries.

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.680
So that's the sort of positive case,

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:49.860
which is that even as the
leaders are doing very little

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:53.050
innovation and falls in
costs of these technology,

00:31:53.050 --> 00:31:56.480
as for the vaccines, have
made it at least feasible

00:31:56.480 --> 00:31:59.030
to talk about working at
the scale that's required.

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:02.463
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:32:02.463 --> 00:32:05.380
And I actually wanted
to bring something up,

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:08.550
ahead of this call Rohini and
I were talking about some of

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.710
the things we wanted to get
into, and I know one thing

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:14.730
she mentioned was carbon
capture and the economics of

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:17.650
carbon capture and linking
to what I thought was a very

00:32:17.650 --> 00:32:19.790
helpful piece that I'll
just quickly reference,

00:32:19.790 --> 00:32:23.170
talking about how stopping
emissions, stopped making

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:25.760
the climate worse, but
repairing the damage,

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:28.140
and so far as repair is
possible will require more than

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:28.973
emissions cuts.

00:32:28.973 --> 00:32:31.230
And that gets to the role
that technology can play,

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:35.050
that Nick was alluding to,
things like carbon removal,

00:32:35.050 --> 00:32:37.250
solar geoengineering.

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:38.830
Rohini, is that something
you wanted to touch on

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:42.400
just in terms of what a fair
deal would look like at COP26,

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:46.280
where technology comes into
this and how it goes beyond just

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:48.080
stopping emissions?

00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:50.140
- So I actually wanted
to pose the question,

00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:54.360
because I'm relatively ignorant
and I felt our panelists

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:55.540
may have something to say on it.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:58.390
But I feel very often right
now, we're still talking about

00:32:58.390 --> 00:32:59.270
cutting emissions.

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:02.290
And so we spend a lot
of time talking about,

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:04.300
renewable energy and issues like that.

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:07.640
But we've already seen the
effects of climate change.

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:10.350
And even if we cut emissions
down to zero today,

00:33:10.350 --> 00:33:12.880
we have all the scars, that remains.

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:16.670
And so I'd be curious to hear
from, I think Sunita, John

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:19.810
and Nick, how should we think
about how soon this is going

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:21.550
to get on the agenda?

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:24.440
And again, how should we
think about a just deal there?

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:27.820
Because that is very clearly
something that you could argue

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:29.950
its historic contributions matter a lot,

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:32.290
when you start thinking
about removing the carbon

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:33.123
in the air.

00:33:35.711 --> 00:33:38.169
So maybe I'll ask Sunita
because you must have

00:33:38.169 --> 00:33:42.067
talked about it, thinking about COP26.

00:33:42.067 --> 00:33:45.040
- So Rohini I think that's
a very fascinating question.

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:47.670
And I think we need to
really work about this,

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:50.477
because yes we are
beyond just saying that,

00:33:50.477 --> 00:33:53.320
we are going to be able
to cut the emissions

00:33:53.320 --> 00:33:54.910
at the scale that is needed.

00:33:54.910 --> 00:33:58.040
And so that's where the
whole concept of net zero

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:02.800
has come in to say, we're going
to live within what would,

00:34:02.800 --> 00:34:05.680
the amount of emissions that we put out,

00:34:05.680 --> 00:34:08.460
we are going to find ways
of being able to soak it up,

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:11.380
capture it, burry it, kill it.

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:12.680
I don't know what else we're going to do.

00:34:12.680 --> 00:34:16.990
We're gonna find some way to
make it magically disappear.

00:34:16.990 --> 00:34:20.430
And if it can disappear,
we will be net zero.

00:34:20.430 --> 00:34:23.800
Now, when you look at the
proposals Rohini, and I think,

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:26.910
I mean, I've been one of the
people opposing net zero,

00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:30.040
and people have often asked
me, "Why would you oppose

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:31.120
such a good idea?"

00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:34.460
I mean, why would anybody
say net zero is bad?

00:34:34.460 --> 00:34:37.440
I mean, one is bad from
an equity point of view.

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:42.440
The fact is IPCC said that we
need to reach net zero by 2050

00:34:42.980 --> 00:34:44.290
for the world.

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:48.540
Now that doesn't mean that
India and the US can have ever

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:49.780
the same timeline.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:53.360
I mean US with its historical emissions

00:34:53.360 --> 00:34:55.610
and if you look at the carbon budget,

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:57.440
I'm not even going to go there.

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:02.330
I think that discussion is
today so over that we shouldn't

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:06.970
even waste our time
beating Donald Trump again.

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:10.660
And try and talk about
how India is equivalent

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:13.753
to the US, so I'm not
even going to go there.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:19.470
But I think the big issue
with net zero is what really

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:23.410
exists to be able to
soak up the emissions.

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:27.290
Now, this is where the concept
of, again, we always keep

00:35:27.290 --> 00:35:30.400
inventing these new words and
then people have to decipher

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:32.287
them and make some sense out of them,

00:35:32.287 --> 00:35:33.860
"nature-based solutions."

00:35:33.860 --> 00:35:35.940
So what is nature based solutions?

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:39.130
We're gonna plant trees and
we already have planted trees

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:40.590
and they're going to soak it up.

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.710
Now, where are we going to plant trees?

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:47.140
The vast forests of the world
and the lands of the world

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:49.560
actually exist in the developing world,

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:52.180
because it's much cheaper to plant there.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:54.103
There is no cost of land.

00:35:55.205 --> 00:35:58.700
We can plant massive
amounts of trees there.

00:35:58.700 --> 00:36:01.920
And that's where the conversation
is going to happen in

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:05.100
Glasgow, which is a
market based mechanism,

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:08.750
bringing this together to say
that countries will be able to

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:12.910
pay for the sequestrations of carbon.

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:16.340
It's a sort of new clean
development mechanism that

00:36:16.340 --> 00:36:18.450
is in the works.

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:20.710
Now, what does all that mean?

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:24.150
And I think that's where
the just transition must and

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:27.910
the whole concept of equity
and justice must be part of it.

00:36:27.910 --> 00:36:31.440
I mean, the biggest issue
with, and I know all of you

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:34.480
will understand this,
biggest issue is land rights.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:37.010
Biggest issue is who controls the land,

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:39.110
who will benefit from it.

00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:41.320
We want to plant trees
in a country like India.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:45.520
We are very clear that we
cannot have only conservation

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:49.240
oriented forestry, we need
development oriented forestry,

00:36:49.240 --> 00:36:53.140
but we also know that that
money must be in the hands of

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:54.260
communities.

00:36:54.260 --> 00:36:57.560
And we also need to understand
the land tenure rights,

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:00.950
where, will communities
benefit from it or not.

00:37:00.950 --> 00:37:03.470
And so I think there're
lot of big issues that

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:06.430
we haven't even begun to understand

00:37:06.430 --> 00:37:09.470
and already and what we need to.

00:37:09.470 --> 00:37:13.100
And again, I'm just saying
this, because I think we need

00:37:13.100 --> 00:37:14.940
to be able to call this out, to say,

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:19.207
we already gotten new
words out there, "net zero"

00:37:19.207 --> 00:37:23.110
"nature-based solutions," all
that sounds as if it's gonna

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:26.363
happen tomorrow, because
these disruptive technologies

00:37:26.363 --> 00:37:30.050
that just going to come in
and everything will disappear.

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:33.600
It's a tough word and we need
to understand that the poor

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:36.790
in the world, Nick talked
about renewable energy.

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:39.480
Let's be clear Nick, on
my part of the world,

00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:43.240
solar is still too expensive
for the poorest in my country.

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:44.073
Okay.

00:37:44.073 --> 00:37:46.950
And let's also be very clear,
yes, the grid has reached

00:37:46.950 --> 00:37:50.500
across India, which is an amazing feat,

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:54.180
but the fact is electricity
has not, because people

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:57.350
are too poor to be able to
pay for that electricity.

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:00.630
And that comes from cheap dirty coal.

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:04.340
So we have to understand
that, and this is India,

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:06.260
then we need to talk about Africa.

00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:10.070
So we need to get some
really hard questions there,

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:13.740
hard issues there, and for
once we should not allow

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:17.480
this discussion to be taken
over with just some nice

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:21.803
genteel words and some nice
things to say and we go home.

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:27.360
- Very powerful call to
action ahead of COP26.

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:29.230
Nick, I wanna bring you into this.

00:38:29.230 --> 00:38:34.230
Sunita mentioned that there's
the rhetoric at COP26,

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:37.633
about net zero, nature based solutions,

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:40.860
these disruptive technologies,
but it seems quite

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:42.780
disconnected from the
reality on the ground.

00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:45.980
And actually mentioned,
solar is still too expensive

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:47.090
for the poorest in India.

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:48.790
So price is a huge part of this.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:51.370
And can you expand on what
you see as the way forward

00:38:51.370 --> 00:38:54.250
and does COP26 get at this,
or is that really perhaps not

00:38:54.250 --> 00:38:56.810
the right forum to find solutions?

00:38:56.810 --> 00:38:59.713
- So it does, but very indirectly.

00:39:00.730 --> 00:39:04.910
And politely, I would
submit, I think that the

00:39:04.910 --> 00:39:09.180
renewable picture is
better than it has been

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:11.060
and it's changing day by day.

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:16.040
So, the numbers in India, the
most recent solar auctions

00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:18.160
are at something like
two and a half rupees,

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:22.830
a kilowatt hour, and most coal
plants have levelized costs

00:39:22.830 --> 00:39:25.800
of three and a half or four rupees.

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:30.370
So it possible that that
energy can actually be cheaper

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:33.360
and thereby enable electrification.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:37.040
Now that needs a lot of, sort
of an enabling environment,

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:39.000
a regulatory environment
to support those things.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:43.140
But the prices are very,
very close, very competitive

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:43.973
nowadays.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:46.674
- Catherine can I just come in?

00:39:46.674 --> 00:39:47.960
- [Catherine] Yes.

00:39:47.960 --> 00:39:51.610
- Nick, slight, let's get the reality.

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:53.420
The fact is, yes, we're all very excited.

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:58.030
I am a real proponent of
renewable and I do believe that's

00:39:58.030 --> 00:40:01.100
the future, but let's sort
of get a reality check.

00:40:01.100 --> 00:40:04.730
The fact is the PLF of the solar is 20%.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:07.900
The PLF of coal is much higher.

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:12.410
So yes, the levelized cost
of solar becomes cheaper,

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:14.740
but that is upfront capital cost.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:18.380
Two, you do not get as many
units from a solar plant

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:20.730
as you get from a coal based plant.

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:25.370
So it is still, and let's be
clear, this is why in India,

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:28.700
we've not seen the commissioning
rate of solar as much as

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:29.700
we'd like.

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:32.480
And this is in spite of the
fact, this is one program

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:35.180
that the government of India
has completely committed to.

00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:40.180
And rightly so, but there is
a basic issue of the ability

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:41.860
to pay for power.

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:45.420
And that's about poverty and
COVID has only made it worse,

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:46.347
not better.

00:40:47.533 --> 00:40:51.620
- And the availability
is certainly a concern

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:54.880
and that's something that gets
worse as the scale of solar

00:40:54.880 --> 00:40:55.713
grows.

00:40:55.713 --> 00:40:58.130
When solar is a small
part of your energy mix,

00:40:58.130 --> 00:40:58.963
it doesn't matter.

00:40:58.963 --> 00:41:03.120
But looking ahead, that's
likely to be a bigger problem.

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:07.350
India is maybe at eight or 10%
renewable generation today.

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:11.360
If that reached to 25 or 30%,
it would make the power grid

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:13.340
much more difficult as well.

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:16.480
- Can I just jump

00:41:16.480 --> 00:41:18.020
in Catherine?
- Go ahead john please.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:22.070
- So earlier Nick suggested
that he was a little bit more

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:25.150
optimistic than some of his
rather pessimistic statistics

00:41:25.150 --> 00:41:26.410
indicated.

00:41:26.410 --> 00:41:31.410
And then with what Sunita
then said later on,

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:34.160
I'm feeling more pessimistic again.

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:37.530
But it strikes me that one
of the framing questions

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:39.200
that we need to be asking as well,

00:41:39.200 --> 00:41:43.870
which everyone's aware of
is the geopolitical aspect.

00:41:43.870 --> 00:41:47.620
Because on the one hand we
have, we're asking some of

00:41:47.620 --> 00:41:51.250
the right questions and
we're actually developing

00:41:51.250 --> 00:41:54.820
some of the correct answers
and we were costing them

00:41:54.820 --> 00:41:58.800
and we can see what they might
achieve if we can apply them,

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:03.020
but actually instrumentalizing
them, it meets a major block.

00:42:03.020 --> 00:42:05.840
On the one hand with geopolitics
and the competition between

00:42:05.840 --> 00:42:10.280
state interests, whatever
the politicians at COP26

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:14.960
might say, they all have to
consider their own particular,

00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:18.380
local political interests
and political survival as

00:42:18.380 --> 00:42:22.260
individuals on the one
hand and on the other,

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.680
we have below the level of states,

00:42:24.680 --> 00:42:29.080
but not really much less
visible, competing global

00:42:29.080 --> 00:42:30.273
multinationals.

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:35.840
We're almost at the stage now
where we have an Asimov like

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.930
world run by giant corporations,

00:42:38.930 --> 00:42:41.020
which we know can have enormous impacts,

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:44.410
particularly on less powerful
states in terms of influencing

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:45.420
them.

00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:48.980
And one only has to think of
the way in which from the point

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:52.160
of view of estate, China
is beginning to affect the

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:55.220
economies of many African countries now,

00:42:55.220 --> 00:42:57.970
both to the advantage of those
countries on the one hand,

00:42:57.970 --> 00:43:00.918
but certainly to the
geo-strategic advantage of

00:43:00.918 --> 00:43:03.600
the People's Republic
of China on the other,

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:08.290
which then complicates the
world geopolitical situation,

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:11.840
increases tensions between
superpowers and so forth.

00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:13.490
We all know that.

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:16.440
How do we build that into
our framework and how do we

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:19.040
address those questions?

00:43:19.040 --> 00:43:21.410
Seems to me to be a fundamental problem.

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:23.240
And from the historian's point of view,

00:43:23.240 --> 00:43:27.333
that's always been even before
people were globally aware,

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:32.340
but let's say in more
recent pre-modernity days

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:35.310
in the 17th and 18th centuries,
when European imperial

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:39.960
states were understanding
the nature of the potentials

00:43:39.960 --> 00:43:42.300
for global power and control.

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:47.300
Even at this point, these
factors were directly impacting

00:43:48.370 --> 00:43:53.370
on local and regional
sustainabilities and their economies.

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:59.540
And I'm wondering the number
of intersecting problems

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:03.390
that we're having to confront
in order to thrash out

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:07.170
some simple policy decisions
and obtain the investment

00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:11.610
we need to contribute towards
that hundreds of trillions

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:16.610
of dollar costs, seems to
me, not quite insurmountable,

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:20.760
but frighteningly problematic.

00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:25.630
And as long as we're
working on a highly divided

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:32.260
nationalistic geopolitical
scenario, that's always going

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:34.080
to be a major sticking point.

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:37.060
Whatever the promises
that might be held out

00:44:37.060 --> 00:44:42.060
to an international conference
or congress such as COP26,

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:46.070
behind that, as soon as the
people who do the speaking

00:44:46.070 --> 00:44:48.140
and make the promises go back home,

00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:50.780
they're confronted by
their own local realities.

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:53.740
And we all have seen on at
least three occasions since the

00:44:53.740 --> 00:44:58.560
1960s, how many of the
promises that were made

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:01.453
were simply written back
on really very rapidly.

00:45:02.600 --> 00:45:05.620
- I wanna bring Nick into this
just because actually Nick

00:45:05.620 --> 00:45:07.990
has to go teach an undergraduate
class in just a moment,

00:45:07.990 --> 00:45:08.990
which we all certainly understand.

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:11.420
But Nick, I saw you nodding
along and I just love any

00:45:11.420 --> 00:45:13.300
reaction you have or just final thoughts.

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.270
And then we'll bring in some
questions from the audience.

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:19.720
- No, I think professor
Haldon's point fundamentally is

00:45:19.720 --> 00:45:23.200
it depends in this pledge and
review in the nature of the

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:25.750
COP on the interest of
the individual countries

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:28.610
and what they will sort of
be able to carry out at home,

00:45:28.610 --> 00:45:32.350
and the US is the worst
example of a laggard,

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:37.350
because its commitments
have always been very modest

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:39.890
if not sort of altogether negligible.

00:45:39.890 --> 00:45:43.540
And yet it's not even lived
up to the commitments it made,

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:46.730
for example, the Green Climate
Fund, which was one of the

00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:50.540
mechanisms conceive to
transfer resources to

00:45:50.540 --> 00:45:52.470
developing countries for adaptation

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:54.770
and mitigation investments,
was supposed to have

00:45:54.770 --> 00:45:57.630
a headline value of a
hundred billion dollars.

00:45:57.630 --> 00:46:01.340
Till date there's been something
like 8 billion committed.

00:46:01.340 --> 00:46:05.470
The US initially offered
three and then gave one.

00:46:05.470 --> 00:46:09.240
So, it's not promising enough
and then not living up to

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:10.290
promises.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:15.150
And so we've reached at odd
point in the COP rounds where,

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:19.240
the sort of historical
countries responsible for mostly

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:23.960
emissions have done very little
and yet India has adopted

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:28.310
a call, has set these ambitious
renewable energy targets.

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:31.500
China though leading the
world in carbon consumption

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:34.650
has also sort of increased
dramatically its investments

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:36.820
in renewable energy and
is supplying the world

00:46:36.820 --> 00:46:38.890
with solar panels to get this done.

00:46:38.890 --> 00:46:42.490
So I think this is a time
where the US and Europe

00:46:42.490 --> 00:46:45.080
in particular need to
sort of walk the walk

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:47.950
and an ounce of walk is
worth a pound of talk

00:46:47.950 --> 00:46:51.950
for the next round to show
that they're actually serious.

00:46:51.950 --> 00:46:53.270
Yeah.

00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:54.980
- Thank you, Nick.

00:46:54.980 --> 00:46:57.010
I wanna go ahead and move
to some questions from our

00:46:57.010 --> 00:46:57.843
audience.

00:46:57.843 --> 00:47:01.510
And this is something we've hinted at,

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:04.040
but this question tackles that head on.

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:07.580
So this is from someone who's
doing a study on climate

00:47:07.580 --> 00:47:12.270
justice and capitalism and
asks whether our speakers could

00:47:12.270 --> 00:47:15.390
speak to the global capitalist
system and whether it

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:17.980
disenfranchises the world's
most vulnerable communities

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.280
in terms of climate justice.

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:22.470
So I want to begin with Rohini and Sunita,

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:25.720
and then perhaps we can go
back in time a little bit

00:47:25.720 --> 00:47:29.230
and talk with John about
really whether addressing

00:47:29.230 --> 00:47:32.240
inequality is necessary
when it comes to solving

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:33.420
the politics of climate change.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:37.290
But first this question on
capitalism and whether it

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:39.890
disenfranchises the world's
most vulnerable in terms of

00:47:39.890 --> 00:47:40.723
climate justice.

00:47:40.723 --> 00:47:42.910
I think it tackles what
we're discussing head on,

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:45.653
and maybe we'll begin with Rohini on that.

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:49.920
- Sure, it's hard to not agree.

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:52.510
So I'm trying to figure out
how do I give a more nuanced

00:47:52.510 --> 00:47:53.980
reply to that?

00:47:53.980 --> 00:47:57.000
So maybe a question think
to about is, why is the

00:47:57.000 --> 00:47:59.510
climate different from all the
other ways in which the poor

00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:02.920
are disenfranchised by the
global capitalist system.

00:48:02.920 --> 00:48:05.460
And again, to go back to one
thing that Sunita talked about,

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:08.120
which is really the
tragedy of the commons.

00:48:08.120 --> 00:48:11.800
So I think Sunita talked about,
thinking about communities

00:48:11.800 --> 00:48:16.150
and forests and it is the case
that the poor the world over

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:20.270
are much more dependent
on agriculture, on land.

00:48:20.270 --> 00:48:24.033
And those are systems
that are particularly,

00:48:25.663 --> 00:48:28.410
one that do particularly badly
if, then there are poorly

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:31.500
defined property rights,
for instance, over water,

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:36.187
or for instance, if someone
can come along and say,

00:48:36.187 --> 00:48:39.470
"I'm going to grow Palm oil
because there's a big market

00:48:39.470 --> 00:48:40.930
for it."

00:48:40.930 --> 00:48:43.300
So, I think perhaps one way
into this question or thinking

00:48:43.300 --> 00:48:46.610
about climate justice is really asked,

00:48:46.610 --> 00:48:48.970
can we start thinking about
the ways in which we give

00:48:48.970 --> 00:48:53.690
legal rights to the world's
poor communities on these lands,

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:55.705
because that's what capitalism asks.

00:48:55.705 --> 00:48:58.090
It asks us to trade in a
marketplace where people have

00:48:58.090 --> 00:48:59.790
rights and they can trade.

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:04.040
And we very often don't
give those depending on

00:49:04.040 --> 00:49:07.280
natural resources, the same
kind of rights as someone say,

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:09.533
buy a house in New Haven.

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:15.030
- Sunita any reactions to
that or this part of question?

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:17.760
- Yeah, no, I think Rohini said it,

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:19.890
I mean, only difference I have is only

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:22.960
some of those rights would be
a little complicated because

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:25.985
it's not just private rights,
it's community rights.

00:49:25.985 --> 00:49:30.985
And the problem with the climate
change and the problem with

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:37.210
forest and grazing lands and
water bodies is the fact that

00:49:37.210 --> 00:49:41.870
we need to redefine the system
of rights and move beyond

00:49:41.870 --> 00:49:43.920
individual rights.

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:47.510
We need individual benefits,
we need cooperation,

00:49:47.510 --> 00:49:51.810
but often rights have to be
held almost like a trust.

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:56.310
And that's where the problem
with the modern capitalistic

00:49:56.310 --> 00:49:59.710
system has been, that it
only understands one kind of

00:49:59.710 --> 00:50:00.543
right.

00:50:00.543 --> 00:50:03.760
And that really is part of the problem.

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:07.540
But I think one of the, and
Catherine I'm also struggling

00:50:07.540 --> 00:50:10.310
and trying to, I don't want
to go down in your panel

00:50:10.310 --> 00:50:13.320
as being the most pessimistic person.

00:50:13.320 --> 00:50:18.020
So I'm also trying to, and
this question gives me a way

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:22.900
to be able to answer, to
say to me, I work in India.

00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:27.900
My entire work is here fighting
for clean air, clean water,

00:50:29.350 --> 00:50:32.510
garbage, forests, all the rest of it.

00:50:32.510 --> 00:50:34.030
And I see progress.

00:50:34.030 --> 00:50:35.742
And so let me be very clear.

00:50:35.742 --> 00:50:40.742
I don't see the same level of
progress that I would like,

00:50:41.920 --> 00:50:43.750
because then I'm an activist.

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:47.380
I want more than I will get at any time,

00:50:47.380 --> 00:50:51.260
but I see more progress in
India than I see globally.

00:50:51.260 --> 00:50:54.650
And I see more progress here
because of the imperative of

00:50:54.650 --> 00:50:55.483
change.

00:50:55.483 --> 00:50:58.510
I mean, we are today
increasingly seeing NIMBY

00:50:58.510 --> 00:51:00.297
on its head.

00:51:00.297 --> 00:51:04.680
"Not in my backyard" has always
been "not in my backyard"

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:06.097
of the rich.

00:51:06.097 --> 00:51:10.940
And it is the poor whose
lands the mark has gone to.

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:15.800
And that is where John
talked about the elite,

00:51:15.800 --> 00:51:19.970
broadly in my view it's the
people who call the shots

00:51:19.970 --> 00:51:23.760
and say, "Not in my backyard
that you have an incinerator,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.570
a coal-based plant, a waste
disposal system, whatever."

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:30.010
It always moves to
somebody else's backyard.

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:34.040
And that's the classic problem
of the way globally we have

00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:37.060
also managed pollution.

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:41.620
And it's not the US ,it's
China which is producing it

00:51:41.620 --> 00:51:42.453
or India.

00:51:42.453 --> 00:51:45.640
And in India, it's produced
because it's so cheap.

00:51:45.640 --> 00:51:46.610
And why is it cheap?

00:51:46.610 --> 00:51:49.850
Because lately any environmental
conditions are given

00:51:51.250 --> 00:51:52.200
a go ahead.

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:54.780
I mean, they don't need to account for it.

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:58.480
Now I see that changing here
because of the fact that you're

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:02.290
getting more and more what I
call "the NIMBY of the poor,"

00:52:02.290 --> 00:52:05.740
where it is the poor who was
saying, "not in my backyard."

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:09.033
Not in my backyard can the
city of Thiruvanthapuram

00:52:09.033 --> 00:52:12.030
and Kerala dump its waste, small thing.

00:52:12.030 --> 00:52:15.730
But the fact is the poor
village woman is saying that,

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:18.620
and the villages are getting
together and saying that,

00:52:18.620 --> 00:52:22.610
and that actually turns the
entire waste system on its head,

00:52:22.610 --> 00:52:25.330
because if the rich can dump
their waste in the lands of the

00:52:25.330 --> 00:52:28.110
poor, they need to dump it
on their own front yard.

00:52:28.110 --> 00:52:30.600
And if they have to dump
it on their own front yard,

00:52:30.600 --> 00:52:32.400
they even have to clean it up.

00:52:32.400 --> 00:52:36.910
So I think to some extent,
this is the power of democracy.

00:52:36.910 --> 00:52:41.270
This is the power of being
able to give a voice to people

00:52:41.270 --> 00:52:44.960
and the rights to people
to be able to ask, demand.

00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:47.630
And that's where the change
is coming in my thought of the

00:52:47.630 --> 00:52:49.678
world that's it.

00:52:49.678 --> 00:52:52.210
So that's the biggest trigger for change,

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:54.210
real change that we are seeing.

00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:56.220
So I see if you can do that globally,

00:52:56.220 --> 00:52:58.890
I don't say it's possible,
but nevertheless,

00:52:58.890 --> 00:53:01.500
I see that as the way ahead.

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:02.940
- Thank you, Sunita.

00:53:02.940 --> 00:53:05.360
We have questions coming in
and we're quickly running

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:09.020
out of time, but John, I
did wanna come back to you,

00:53:09.020 --> 00:53:11.250
not necessarily to talk
about the capitalist system

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:16.160
as it exists today, but this
connection between inequality

00:53:16.160 --> 00:53:20.470
and the politics of climate
change and how we can make

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:23.990
changes and design institutions
that address, not just

00:53:23.990 --> 00:53:25.773
climate change but inequality.

00:53:27.312 --> 00:53:30.990
- I think the first thing
is that if one looks at the

00:53:30.990 --> 00:53:34.230
capitalist system as
a global system today,

00:53:34.230 --> 00:53:38.930
then it is a deeply oppressive
system and an excluding

00:53:38.930 --> 00:53:41.220
system in many respects.

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:46.220
And in theory, one can theorize
a capitalist world system

00:53:49.070 --> 00:53:51.500
that is less inegalitarian.

00:53:53.200 --> 00:53:58.200
But I think by its very nature,
that sort of market economic

00:53:59.030 --> 00:54:04.030
system requires the necessity
for some people to be kept

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:10.570
if you like, at the
level of being excluded

00:54:11.410 --> 00:54:13.670
in some respects, from a
range of different types

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:17.040
of resource and access to
resource and facilities.

00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:20.120
On the other hand, it also
depends upon consumerism.

00:54:20.120 --> 00:54:23.380
And one of the major problems
that we, it wasn't mentioned

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:28.380
earlier, but it's a key
issue, is the mass consumerism

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:31.730
of the privileged parts of the world.

00:54:31.730 --> 00:54:36.610
Which is one of the major
drivers for both air pollution

00:54:36.610 --> 00:54:38.343
and industrial output.

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:41.570
So it has both good and bad sides,

00:54:41.570 --> 00:54:44.860
how to square that circle,
that's a whole different issue.

00:54:44.860 --> 00:54:49.590
Do we need to get rid of the elites?

00:54:49.590 --> 00:54:52.640
Well, it's a nice idea I
suppose, if one isn't a member

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:53.473
of the elite.

00:54:55.670 --> 00:54:59.630
But we'll always need to
either choose, elect, select,

00:54:59.630 --> 00:55:03.720
or somehow have people who
can speak for the larger

00:55:03.720 --> 00:55:06.900
majority, however they're
selected or elected.

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:09.960
And that takes us to a whole
different ball game about

00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:11.480
political systems and so forth,

00:55:11.480 --> 00:55:15.053
which we probably shouldn't get into.

00:55:16.900 --> 00:55:20.620
I think what one can aim for
though, I mean, the real issue,

00:55:20.620 --> 00:55:23.800
which I've mentioned in our
private discussions before,

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:25.600
is baseline inequality.

00:55:25.600 --> 00:55:29.170
What makes societies in the
past least able to resist

00:55:29.170 --> 00:55:32.120
substantial environmental challenge.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:35.270
And the greater the degree
of baseline inequality,

00:55:35.270 --> 00:55:38.220
the less resilient and the
less sustainable they turn out

00:55:38.220 --> 00:55:39.053
to be.

00:55:39.053 --> 00:55:41.420
It doesn't mean to say
that in the short term,

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:44.560
some elements of those societies
don't do very well out of

00:55:44.560 --> 00:55:47.750
change, and those elements
are usually elites.

00:55:47.750 --> 00:55:51.310
But it does mean that the greater
the distribution of wealth

00:55:51.310 --> 00:55:54.000
across the society, even
if you have an elite

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:58.850
and an underprivileged group
at the other end of society,

00:55:58.850 --> 00:56:02.130
the greater the degree of
distribution of wealth,

00:56:02.130 --> 00:56:05.240
the more flexible, the more
resilient, the less brittle

00:56:05.240 --> 00:56:08.610
a societal system tends to be.

00:56:08.610 --> 00:56:12.130
And that means it can overcome challenges,

00:56:12.130 --> 00:56:15.200
quite substantial challenges,
both short and long term

00:56:15.200 --> 00:56:16.120
more successfully.

00:56:16.120 --> 00:56:18.460
Again, there are always
costs to resilience.

00:56:18.460 --> 00:56:20.993
You can't have resilience
without paying a cost.

00:56:23.230 --> 00:56:27.210
That's the basic message that
certainly we learned from

00:56:27.210 --> 00:56:28.580
the historical record.

00:56:28.580 --> 00:56:32.090
Maybe in the future that can
be different if we organize

00:56:32.090 --> 00:56:34.990
our resources and access
to resources differently

00:56:34.990 --> 00:56:36.440
from the way we currently do.

00:56:37.590 --> 00:56:41.390
So I think maybe getting rid
of elites is a nice idea,

00:56:41.390 --> 00:56:43.870
but it's not likely to
happen anytime soon.

00:56:43.870 --> 00:56:46.240
But what we can do, and we
can demonstrate that this

00:56:46.240 --> 00:56:51.240
is possible in certain
sectors of the world today,

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:55.900
is have a much better
distribution of social wealth

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:58.130
and access to social wealth and resources

00:56:58.130 --> 00:57:00.550
than we currently have in many countries.

00:57:00.550 --> 00:57:03.150
And it's that extreme
difference between the very,

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:07.350
very small, super wealthy
elite and a much larger,

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:10.470
massive people who are more
or less entirely excluded from

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:14.040
the possibility of achieving
access to the resources

00:57:14.040 --> 00:57:18.600
that make for a healthy
and sustainable life.

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:21.320
If we can get rid of that difference,

00:57:21.320 --> 00:57:24.590
we already take a
substantial step forward.

00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:25.423
- Thank you John.

00:57:25.423 --> 00:57:27.530
I wanna end with a
question from the audience

00:57:27.530 --> 00:57:30.620
and perhaps even broaden
the lens of this question.

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:32.140
'Cause I think it's something
we should all consider

00:57:32.140 --> 00:57:34.370
and leaders at COP26 should consider.

00:57:34.370 --> 00:57:38.080
But the question is, what
are steps you think youth can

00:57:38.080 --> 00:57:41.810
or should take to engage in
climate activism in a more

00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:45.280
class/wealth conscious way?

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:49.500
So how can youth, or how
can we all take steps

00:57:49.500 --> 00:57:52.250
to when we think about climate,
think of it in terms of

00:57:52.250 --> 00:57:55.190
a fair deal, based on all
of the dynamics we discussed

00:57:55.190 --> 00:57:56.023
today.

00:57:56.023 --> 00:57:57.380
So with just a couple
minutes left quickly,

00:57:57.380 --> 00:58:00.030
I'll go to Sunita and John, and
then we'll wrap with Rohini.

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:02.280
But Sunita, any reactions
to that great question?

00:58:02.280 --> 00:58:04.250
- Yeah, I just want to say Catherine,

00:58:04.250 --> 00:58:09.240
I'm so impressed by the
words of the youth of today.

00:58:09.240 --> 00:58:14.240
And I have to say that I
have been a climate activist

00:58:14.590 --> 00:58:19.300
since 1990 and it has
been a very tough job.

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:22.820
And I have literally been
in tears in many meetings

00:58:22.820 --> 00:58:27.550
to try and get the word of
equity, justice and to get people

00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:29.780
to understand why it is important.

00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:34.780
And I still believe the
establishment, NGO who work,

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:39.430
civil society who works on
this, I fail to get them

00:58:39.430 --> 00:58:40.307
to understand.

00:58:40.307 --> 00:58:43.790
But when I see the voices
of the youth today,

00:58:43.790 --> 00:58:47.470
they understand, they're
rooted in the politics

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:49.070
of climate change.

00:58:49.070 --> 00:58:53.350
And I'm seeing a diversity of
voices from the youth as well.

00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:57.190
So I think hats off to the
youth of the world today,

00:58:57.190 --> 00:59:00.230
not only are they talking to the power,

00:59:00.230 --> 00:59:04.860
but they aren't rooting
the conversation on climate

00:59:04.860 --> 00:59:07.213
in the reality of climate,
in the politics of climate.

00:59:08.705 --> 00:59:11.900
And that is critical for us to move ahead.

00:59:11.900 --> 00:59:15.990
So I would only say, do
more of what you're doing.

00:59:15.990 --> 00:59:16.823
- Thank you Sunita.

00:59:16.823 --> 00:59:18.930
And I believe that question
came from a Yale undergrad.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:19.763
So thank you for that.

00:59:19.763 --> 00:59:21.890
John, any reaction to that?

00:59:21.890 --> 00:59:26.840
- Well, yeah, I think younger
people have really set the,

00:59:26.840 --> 00:59:31.250
established an initiative
and I can't ever claim to be

00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:33.250
a climate activist as such,
although I've always been

00:59:33.250 --> 00:59:36.140
politically active and I
guess nowadays the two things

00:59:36.140 --> 00:59:39.440
have to overlap to a very large extent.

00:59:39.440 --> 00:59:43.280
But I think some of the movements
that one sees such as this

00:59:43.280 --> 00:59:46.770
Extinction Rebellion some of the other

00:59:46.770 --> 00:59:50.430
youth based movements, but
which have also attracted

00:59:50.430 --> 00:59:55.430
a lot of support from older generations

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:58.310
and which are cross social class as well,

00:59:58.310 --> 01:00:01.890
the people involved are from
working, class middle class

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:06.890
backgrounds, of course
different cultures define social

01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:10.800
and cultural class in different ways.

01:00:10.800 --> 01:00:12.810
When one talks about
middle class in America,

01:00:12.810 --> 01:00:14.740
it means something quite different
from when one talks about

01:00:14.740 --> 01:00:17.140
middle-class in England, for example.

01:00:17.140 --> 01:00:21.080
Nevertheless, the activism I think spreads

01:00:21.080 --> 01:00:23.390
and it's contagious,
and it's a good thing.

01:00:23.390 --> 01:00:26.030
And we have to accept
that activism has to be a

01:00:26.030 --> 01:00:26.863
broad church.

01:00:26.863 --> 01:00:28.130
There are gonna be some aspects of it,

01:00:28.130 --> 01:00:30.333
which we as individuals might not like,

01:00:32.414 --> 01:00:35.350
and other aspects which we
feel are not activist enough.

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:39.600
But the crucial point is to
make an impact and to force

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:43.120
people to pay more attention
to the problems and issues

01:00:43.120 --> 01:00:43.953
that we face.

01:00:43.953 --> 01:00:46.790
And that's what political
activism has always been about.

01:00:46.790 --> 01:00:51.790
It hasn't necessarily been to
directly bring about a change

01:00:52.060 --> 01:00:53.050
in itself.

01:00:53.050 --> 01:00:55.910
It's been about attracting
enough attention to persuade

01:00:55.910 --> 01:00:58.690
other people to get involved
and realizes an issue

01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:02.210
and do something about it,
either in their voting patterns

01:01:02.210 --> 01:01:05.600
or in their daily lives or
whatever it happens to be.

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:06.567
- Thank you, John.

01:01:06.567 --> 01:01:08.440
And Rohini, your thoughts
on this, to close this out.

01:01:08.440 --> 01:01:10.510
- I know we're out of time,
so I'll keep it short.

01:01:10.510 --> 01:01:13.260
So I think I would just say
something that was perhaps

01:01:13.260 --> 01:01:15.800
implicit in what John was saying, which is

01:01:17.950 --> 01:01:20.760
you can't ignore the state
or the politics of the state

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:21.593
in the end.

01:01:21.593 --> 01:01:25.090
So I think a lot of activism
is great and groups like

01:01:25.090 --> 01:01:27.230
Extinction Rebellion are
great, but it reminds me

01:01:27.230 --> 01:01:30.533
in development, we've had a
long history, in fact, even at

01:01:30.533 --> 01:01:33.040
Yale, like these are the
most active student groups as

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:34.730
effective altruists.

01:01:34.730 --> 01:01:36.940
And I think the thing I always
tell them is, "You have to

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:37.910
engage with the state."

01:01:37.910 --> 01:01:41.380
I mean, in the end, it isn't
about, there isn't that much

01:01:41.380 --> 01:01:43.130
foreign aid in the world
for poor countries.

01:01:43.130 --> 01:01:45.410
It's about getting them to
get their state to work.

01:01:45.410 --> 01:01:48.820
So I think the youth are doing
great things and they should

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:51.080
just make sure they vote
and the push for change

01:01:51.080 --> 01:01:52.880
within the political system as well.

01:01:54.130 --> 01:01:55.140
- Thank you Rohini.

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:56.010
We are out of time.

01:01:56.010 --> 01:01:58.020
I wanna thank you all
so much for joining us.

01:01:58.020 --> 01:02:00.587
Again, this conversation
is part of a larger series,

01:02:00.587 --> 01:02:02.080
"The Yale Development Dialogues,"

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:04.600
and our next event will
focus on health equity.

01:02:04.600 --> 01:02:06.960
It'll take place on
Thursday, October 28th.

01:02:06.960 --> 01:02:09.090
So stay tuned for details on that.

01:02:09.090 --> 01:02:11.720
Thank you to our co-hosts the
Yale Economic Growth Center,

01:02:11.720 --> 01:02:14.280
Jackson Institute for Global
Affairs and History Department.

01:02:14.280 --> 01:02:16.500
Thank you so much to our
panelists and thanks to all of you

01:02:16.500 --> 01:02:17.333
for joining.

